From b29afa5e97ba6a8369afe9074566404b175f5923 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jackwang0009 <93422958+jackwang0009@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 13:23:09 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] [upstream-candidate] Do not leak graphics state inside a text
 clip group

Every object is written between a `q` and a `Q` of its own, and only
the parameters that differ from the initial graphics state are written.
The texts of a text clip group are the exception: they share one `q`,
and that `q` stays open while the objects they clip are written. What a
text of the group set was still set for the texts after it and for the
clipped objects, and nothing was written for those when their value was
the initial one. A text with `2 Tc` followed by one with `0 Tc` came out
as two texts with `2 Tc`; the same went for Tw, the line width, cap,
join, miter limit, dash pattern, flatness and rendering intent, and for
alpha and blend mode when they were changed through the API.

Keep track of the parameters that were written inside the `q`s that are
still open (WrittenState), and write those whatever their value. When
alpha or blend mode were set with a dictionary of the generator's own,
the default graphics dictionary goes first for the objects that follow.
Nothing changes for content without text clip groups, nor for a group
whose texts do not set any of these parameters.

Tests: CPDFPageContentGeneratorTest.GraphicsStateInTextClipGroup.
pdfium_unittests and pdfium_embeddertests: no new failures.

Verification (ops_diff.py): no change, the samples do not have such
content. No-diff pages 65/69 -> 65/69 synthetic, 384/388 -> 384/388
public.

Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
